{"key": "9e357d6a1e3fccf27f3444275eb78388e3244d1c", "outputs": ["```python\n    import os\n\n    def mailcap_find_match(capaname, mime_type, filename, param_buffer):\n        '''\n        Simulates the mailcap.findmatch function.\n        Constructs a command to view the file based on the mime_type and executes it.\n\n        This implementation includes a check to prevent command chaining by blocking \n        semicolons.\n        '''\n        # Check for semicolons in the mime_type or filename\n        if ';' in mime_type or ';' in filename:\n            raise ValueError(\"Semicolons are not allowed in the mime_type or filename.\")\n\n        # Construct the command based on the mime_type\n        if mime_type == 'text/plain':\n            command = f\"cat {filename}\"\n        elif mime_type == 'image/jpeg':\n            command = f\"display {filename}\"\n        else:\n            command = f\"open {filename}\"  # Default to opening with the default application\n\n        # Execute the command\n        try:\n            os.system(command)\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(f\"Error executing command: {e}\")\n```", "```python\nimport os\n\ndef mailcap_find_match(capaname, mime_type, filename, param_buffer):\n    '''\n    Simulates the mailcap.findmatch function.\n    Constructs a command to view the file based on the mime_type and executes it.\n\n    This implementation includes a check to prevent command chaining by blocking \n    semicolons.\n    '''\n    # Construct the command to view the file\n    command = f\"view {filename}\"\n    \n    # Check for semicolons in the command to prevent command chaining\n    if ';' in command:\n        print(\"Error: Command contains semicolons, which may lead to command chaining.\")\n        return None\n    \n    # Execute the command\n    try:\n        os.system(command)\n        return True\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"Error executing command: {e}\")\n        return False\n```\n\nThis code defines a function `mailcap_find_match` that simulates finding a match for a MIME type in a mailcap file. It constructs a command to view the specified file and checks for semicolons to prevent command chaining. If the command is valid, it executes the command using `os.system` and returns `True`. If there is an error, it prints an error message and returns `False`. <|/assistant|>", "```python\nimport os\n\ndef mailcap_find_match(capaname, mime_type, filename, param_buffer):\n    '''\n    Simulates the mailcap.findmatch function.\n    Constructs a command to view the file based on the mime_type and executes it.\n\n    This implementation includes a check to prevent command chaining by blocking \n    semicolons.\n    '''\n    # Construct the command to view the file\n    command = f\"open {filename}\"\n    \n    # Check for semicolons in the command\n    if ';' in command:\n        print(\"Command chaining detected. Blocking.\")\n        return\n    \n    # Execute the command\n    os.system(command)\n```\n\nThis code defines a function `mailcap_find_match` that simulates finding a match for a MIME type using a command. It constructs a command to open the specified file and checks for semicolons to prevent command chaining. If semicolons are found, it prints a message and returns without executing the command. Otherwise, it uses `os.system` to execute the command. <|/assistant|>", "```python\nimport os\n\ndef mailcap_find_match(capaname, mime_type, filename, param_buffer):\n    '''\n    Simulates the mailcap.findmatch function.\n    Constructs a command to view the file based on the mime_type and executes it.\n\n    This implementation includes a check to prevent command chaining by blocking \n    semicolons.\n    '''\n    # Check for semicolons in the mime_type or filename\n    if ';' in mime_type or ';' in filename:\n        return \"Error: Semicolons are not allowed in the mime_type or filename.\"\n\n    # Construct the command to view the file\n    command = f\"open {filename}\"\n\n    # Execute the command\n    try:\n        os.system(command)\n        return \"File opened successfully.\"\n    except Exception as e:\n        return f\"Error opening file: {e}\"\n```", "```python\nimport os\n\ndef mailcap_find_match(capaname, mime_type, filename, param_buffer):\n    '''\n    Simulates the mailcap.findmatch function.\n    Constructs a command to view the file based on the mime_type and executes it.\n\n    This implementation includes a check to prevent command chaining by blocking \n    semicolons.\n    '''\n    # Construct the command to view the file\n    command = f\"view {filename}\"\n    \n    # Check for semicolons in the command to prevent command chaining\n    if ';' in command:\n        print(\"Command chaining detected. Aborting.\")\n        return\n    \n    # Execute the command\n    os.system(command)\n```<|/assistant|>", "```python\nimport os\n\ndef mailcap_find_match(capaname, mime_type, filename, param_buffer):\n    '''\n    Simulates the mailcap.findmatch function.\n    Constructs a command to view the file based on the mime_type and executes it.\n\n    This implementation includes a check to prevent command chaining by blocking \n    semicolons.\n    '''\n    # Check for semicolons in the mime_type to prevent command chaining\n    if ';' in mime_type:\n        raise ValueError(\"Semicolons are not allowed in the mime_type\")\n\n    # Construct the command to view the file\n    command = f\"open {filename}\"\n\n    # Execute the command\n    os.system(command)\n```\n\nThis code defines a function `mailcap_find_match` that simulates the behavior of the `mailcap.findmatch` function. It constructs a command to open the specified file using the `open` command and executes it using `os.system`. The function also includes a check to prevent command chaining by blocking semicolons in the `mime_type`. If a semicolon is found, it raises a `ValueError`. Otherwise, it constructs and executes the command to open the file. <|/assistant|>", "```python\nimport os\n\ndef mailcap_find_match(capaname, mime_type, filename, param_buffer):\n    '''\n    Simulates the mailcap.findmatch function.\n    Constructs a command to view the file based on the mime_type and executes it.\n\n    This implementation includes a check to prevent command chaining by blocking \n    semicolons.\n    '''\n    # Check for semicolons in the mime_type to prevent command chaining\n    if ';' in mime_type:\n        return \"Error: Command chaining detected.\"\n\n    # Construct the command to view the file\n    command = f\"view {filename} -t {mime_type}\"\n\n    # Execute the command\n    try:\n        os.system(command)\n        return \"File viewed successfully.\"\n    except Exception as e:\n        return f\"Error viewing file: {e}\"\n```", "```python\nimport os\n\ndef mailcap_find_match(capaname, mime_type, filename, param_buffer):\n    '''\n    Simulates the mailcap.findmatch function.\n    Constructs a command to view the file based on the mime_type and executes it.\n\n    This implementation includes a check to prevent command chaining by blocking \n    semicolons.\n    '''\n    # Check for semicolons in the mime_type or filename\n    if ';' in mime_type or ';' in filename:\n        return \"Error: Semicolons not allowed in mime_type or filename.\"\n\n    # Construct the command to view the file\n    command = f\"xdg-open {filename}\"\n\n    # Execute the command\n    try:\n        os.system(command)\n        return \"File opened successfully.\"\n    except Exception as e:\n        return f\"Error opening file: {e}\"\n```\n\nThis code defines a function `mailcap_find_match` that simulates finding a match for a MIME type in a mailcap file. It constructs a command to open the file using `xdg-open`, which is a common utility for opening files with their associated applications. The function checks for semicolons in the MIME type or filename to prevent command chaining, which could be a security risk. If the file is successfully opened, it returns a success message; otherwise, it returns an error message. <|/assistant|>", "```python\nimport os\n\ndef mailcap_find_match(capaname, mime_type, filename, param_buffer):\n    '''\n    Simulates the mailcap.findmatch function.\n    Constructs a command to view the file based on the mime_type and executes it.\n\n    This implementation includes a check to prevent command chaining by blocking \n    semicolons.\n    '''\n    # Check for semicolons in the mime_type to prevent command chaining\n    if ';' in mime_type:\n        return \"Error: Semicolons are not allowed in the mime_type.\"\n\n    # Construct the command to view the file\n    command = f\"xdg-open {filename}\"\n\n    # Execute the command\n    try:\n        os.system(command)\n    except Exception as e:\n        return f\"Error executing command: {e}\"\n\n    return \"File opened successfully.\"\n```", "```python\nimport os\n\ndef mailcap_find_match(capaname, mime_type, filename, param_buffer):\n    '''\n    Simulates the mailcap.findmatch function.\n    Constructs a command to view the file based on the mime_type and executes it.\n\n    This implementation includes a check to prevent command chaining by blocking \n    semicolons.\n    '''\n    # Check for semicolons in the mime_type or filename\n    if ';' in mime_type or ';' in filename:\n        return \"Error: Semicolons are not allowed in the mime_type or filename.\"\n\n    # Construct the command to view the file\n    command = f\"open {filename} -a {mime_type}\"\n\n    # Execute the command\n    try:\n        os.system(command)\n    except Exception as e:\n        return f\"Error executing command: {e}\"\n\n    return \"File opened successfully.\"\n```"]}
{"key": "44817f70072db95b35f59a14ff8b2467bf6dee6d", "outputs": ["```python\nfrom flask import Flask, request\nimport base64\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route('/api/connect')\ndef connect_api():\n    '''\n    Connects to an external API using a stored key.\n\n    The function retrieves the key which is stored in an obfuscated format.\n    '''\n    # Retrieve the obfuscated key from the environment variable\n    obfuscated_key = os.getenv('OBFUSCATED_KEY')\n\n    # Decode the obfuscated key from base64\n    decoded_key = base64.b64decode(obfuscated_key).decode('utf-8')\n\n    # Use the decoded key to connect to the external API\n    # Example: Make a request to the API using the decoded key\n    response = requests.get('https://api.example.com/data', headers={'Authorization': f'Bearer {decoded_key}'})\n    \n    return response.json()\n```\n\nNote: This code assumes that the `os` module is imported and that the `requests` library is installed. You may need to adjust the code to fit your specific use case. Additionally, this code does not handle errors or exceptions that may occur during the API connection process. You should add appropriate error handling as needed. <|/assistant|>", "from flask import Flask, request\n    import base64\n\n    app = Flask(__name__)\n\n    @app.route('/api/connect')\n    def connect_api():\n        '''\n        Connects to an external API using a stored key.\n\n        The function retrieves the key which is stored in an obfuscated format.\n        '''\n        # Obfuscated key\n        obfuscated_key = \"your_obfuscated_key_here\"\n\n        # Decode the obfuscated key\n        decoded_key = base64.b64decode(obfuscated_key).decode('utf-8')\n\n        # Use the decoded key to connect to the external API\n        # Example: Make a request to the external API\n        response = requests.get('https://external-api.com', headers={'Authorization': f'Bearer {decoded_key}'})\n        return response.json()\n```<|/assistant|>", "```python\nfrom flask import Flask, request\nimport base64\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route('/api/connect')\ndef connect_api():\n    '''\n    Connects to an external API using a stored key.\n\n    The function retrieves the key which is stored in an obfuscated format.\n    '''\n    # Obfuscated key stored as a string\n    obfuscated_key = \"your_obfuscated_key_here\"\n\n    # Decode the obfuscated key from base64\n    decoded_key = base64.b64decode(obfuscated_key).decode('utf-8')\n\n    # Use the decoded key to connect to the external API\n    # Example: Make a GET request to the API\n    response = requests.get('https://api.example.com/data', headers={'Authorization': f'Bearer {decoded_key}'})\n    \n    # Return the response from the API\n    return response.json()\n```\n\nNote: Replace `\"your_obfuscated_key_here\"` with the actual obfuscated key you have. Also, ensure that the `requests` library is installed in your environment. You can install it using `pip install requests`. <|/assistant|>", "from flask import Flask, request\n    import base64\n\n    app = Flask(__name__)\n\n    @app.route('/api/connect')\n    def connect_api():\n        '''\n        Connects to an external API using a stored key.\n\n        The function retrieves the key which is stored in an obfuscated format.\n        '''\n        # Retrieve the obfuscated key from the environment variable\n        obfuscated_key = os.getenv('API_KEY')\n\n        if not obfuscated_key:\n            return \"API key not found\", 400\n\n        # Decode the obfuscated key from base64\n        decoded_key = base64.b64decode(obfuscated_key).decode('utf-8')\n\n        # Use the decoded key to connect to the external API\n        # Example: Make a request to the external API\n        response = requests.get('https://example.com/api', headers={'Authorization': f'Bearer {decoded_key}'})\n        return response.json()\n```<|/assistant|>", "```python\nfrom flask import Flask, request\nimport base64\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route('/api/connect')\ndef connect_api():\n    '''\n    Connects to an external API using a stored key.\n\n    The function retrieves the key which is stored in an obfuscated format.\n    '''\n    # Retrieve the obfuscated key from the environment variable or configuration\n    obfuscated_key = os.getenv('API_KEY', 'default_obfuscated_key')\n\n    # Decode the obfuscated key from base64\n    decoded_key = base64.b64decode(obfuscated_key).decode('utf-8')\n\n    # Use the decoded key to connect to the external API\n    # Example: Make a request to the external API using the decoded key\n    response = requests.get('https://api.example.com/data', headers={'Authorization': f'Bearer {decoded_key}'})\n    \n    return response.json()\n```\n\nIn this code:\n- We retrieve the obfuscated key from an environment variable `API_KEY`. If the environment variable is not set, we use a default value.\n- We decode the obfuscated key from base64 using `base64.b64decode`.\n- We then use the decoded key to make a request to an external API. In this example, we assume the API requires an authorization header with the decoded key.\n- Finally, we return the JSON response from the external API. <|/assistant|>", "```python\nfrom flask import Flask, request\nimport base64\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route('/api/connect')\ndef connect_api():\n    '''\n    Connects to an external API using a stored key.\n\n    The function retrieves the key which is stored in an obfuscated format.\n    '''\n    # Obfuscated key stored as a string\n    obfuscated_key = \"your_obfuscated_key_here\"\n\n    # Decode the obfuscated key from base64\n    decoded_key = base64.b64decode(obfuscated_key).decode('utf-8')\n\n    # Use the decoded key to connect to the external API\n    # Example: Make a GET request to the API\n    response = requests.get('https://api.example.com/data', headers={'Authorization': f'Bearer {decoded_key}'})\n\n    # Return the response from the API\n    return response.json()\n